/* =====================================================================
   064_iso27001_v13_event_response.sql

   Bulk load of the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Event Response Obligation register, v1.3.

   Source     : Import Practice & Obligation Mapping v1.3 (4).xlsx, sheet 'Event Response Register'
   Rows       : 1194 Event Response Obligations

   Writes to:
     GRAC_New.requirement_obligation             parent row, one per sheet row
     GRAC_New.obligation_event_response   Event Response detail
     GRAC_New.requirement_obligation_evidence    one evidence spec per Obligation
     GRAC_New.obligation_event_response_evidence_link    Event Response -> evidence link
     GRAC_New.obligation_requirement_release_map Practice + Release + Statement link
     GRAC_New.obligation_framework_statement_map Obligation -> Statement (056)

   Prerequisites : 026 (taxonomy schema), 027 (type master seed),
                   033 (event types), 052 (statements), 055 (obligation
                   description), 056 (obligation -> statement map),
                   058 (reset), 059 (masters), 060 (Practices).
                   060 MUST have run: every Obligation resolves its
                   Practice by Practice Name.
   Rerunnable    : yes.  An Obligation whose (Practice, Obligation Name)
                   already exists is skipped, and a partially loaded one is
                   repaired -- see "HOW AN OBLIGATION IS IDENTIFIED".
   Rollback      : 064_iso27001_v13_event_response_rollback.sql

   FILE ENCODING
   -------------
   Saved as UTF-8 with BOM.  Several texts contain U+2019 (right single
   quotation mark, as in "organisation's").  Open in SSMS normally, or run
   with: sqlcmd -f 65001 -i 064_iso27001_v13_event_response.sql
   All literals are N'' prefixed and embedded apostrophes are doubled.

   HOW AN OBLIGATION IS IDENTIFIED
   -------------------------------
   By the pair (Practice, Obligation Name) -- NOT by Obligation Name alone.

   requirement_obligation has no code column, so a natural key has to come
   out of the sheet.  Obligation Name on its own will not do it: 22 names
   are reused across the five registers, and six are reused inside this
   workbook's own registers.  The pair is unique -- all 12,071 rows across
   all five registers produce 12,071 distinct (Practice ID, Practice Name,
   Obligation Name) triples -- and validation step 5 re-proves it for this
   register before anything is written.

   Because the key is a pair, the MERGE below captures each inserted
   obligation_id against its staging row number rather than looking the row
   back up by name afterwards, which is what an OUTPUT-then-join-by-name
   would have to do and what a duplicated name would break.

   #key is then topped up with the obligation_ids of rows that already
   existed, so a re-run after a partial failure repairs the missing detail,
   evidence and mapping rows instead of skipping the Obligation whole.

   HOW A STATEMENT IS RESOLVED
   ---------------------------
   Not by looking the control up again.  Each Obligation inherits the
   Statement from the Practice mapping 060 already wrote:
   framework_statement_requirement_map gives the (Practice -> Statement)
   pair, and this script records that same Statement on the Obligation's
   own mapping row.  Whichever resolution mode 060 ran in, this follows it.

   The register sheets carry no control column of their own, so the
   expected statement_reference for each row was resolved from the
   Practices sheet at generation time and staged in #obl.  Validation
   step 6 checks it against the Statement inherited from 060's mapping, so
   a Practice that ended up under the wrong control stops the load rather
   than propagating into 12,071 Obligation mappings.

   OBLIGATION TYPE
   ---------------
   Every row is 'Event Response'.  obligation_type_id is resolved from
   obligation_type_master by type_code -- 'EventResponse' -- and asserted
   before the load; the sheet's own 'Obligation Type' column is not
   trusted as the key.

   The code and the label are not always the same string.  027 seeds the
   Event Response type as type_code 'EventResponse' with type_name
   'Event Response', so a lookup written against the label finds nothing
   and the load stops on validation step 1.  This script looks up by
   'EventResponse' and uses 'Event Response' only where a human-readable label is
   wanted -- the audit trail and the load report.

   EVENT RESPONSE DETAIL
   ---------------------
     Trigger Event   -> obligation_event_response.trigger_event   (NOT NULL)
     Response Action -> obligation_event_response.response_action (NOT NULL)
     Escalation Path -> escalation_path
     SLA Value/Unit  -> remarks (see below)

   WHY sla_value AND sla_unit ARE LEFT NULL
   ----------------------------------------
   obligation_event_response.sla_value is INT and sla_unit is constrained
   by ck_cm_obligation_event_response_sla_unit to
   'Hours','Days','Weeks','Months','Years'.

   The workbook's SLA Value is a sentence on every one of the 1,194 rows,
   not a number -- 'Within the risk-based review period', 'Immediately for
   active risk; otherwise within the approved treatment time'.  Not one row
   parses as an integer.  SLA Unit is the literal 'Not specified in source'
   on 1,193 rows and 'Business days' on the remaining one, which is not in
   the constraint's list either.

   Forcing a number out of that text would invent a service target the
   source does not state.  So both columns stay NULL and the sheet's exact
   wording is preserved verbatim in obligation_event_response.remarks,
   where nothing is lost and nothing is fabricated.  Validation step 7
   re-proves that no staged SLA value is numeric, so a future workbook
   that does supply real numbers fails loudly here instead of silently
   discarding them.

   ESCALATION PATH
   ---------------
   1,193 of the 1,194 rows carry the literal 'Not specified in source'.
   That is an absence, not a value, so those stage as NULL -- the staging
   block below writes NULL for them.  The single row that names real
   escalation roles keeps its text.

   EVIDENCE
   --------
     Evidence Type        -> requirement_obligation_evidence.evidence_type_id,
                             resolved by name against evidence_type_master.
                             The ten names this workbook uses are seeded by
                             059; validation step 7 proves they all resolve.
     Evidence Description -> requirement_obligation_evidence.remarks
     Retention Period     -> blank on every row in this workbook, so
                             retention_requirement is left NULL.

   026 kept requirement_obligation_evidence as the single evidence store;
   the per-type link table (obligation_event_response_evidence_link) records which
   taxonomy branch owns it.  Both are written here.

   MAKER-CHECKER
   -------------
   'obligations' is registered with is_maker_checker = 1, so interactive
   saves route through change_management.  This script is a controlled bulk
   load and writes directly, exactly as 052, 053 and 054 do.  An
   audit_trace row is written per inserted Obligation.

   COLUMN MAPPING WORTH KNOWING
   ----------------------------
     Obligation Name -> requirement_obligation.obligation_name
     Description     -> requirement_obligation.obligation_text  (needs 055)
     Keywords        -> requirement_obligation.keywords         (needs 023)
     load provenance -> requirement_obligation.remarks

   055 bound the Obligation Master form's Description box to obligation_text
   and stopped the save path from clobbering it.  Before 055 a description
   written here survived only until somebody opened the Obligation and
   clicked Save.  The preflight below checks for it.

   NOT SUPPLIED BY THE SHEET
   -------------------------
   retention_requirement : blank in the workbook.  Left NULL.
   evidence frequency    : left NULL on requirement_obligation_evidence.
   severity / mandatory  : not on requirement_obligation.  n/a.
   ===================================================================== */

SET NOCOUNT ON;
SET XACT_ABORT ON;
GO

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------
-- Clear stale temp tables -- SEPARATE BATCH, ON PURPOSE
--
-- # temp tables live for the whole session, not the script.  If an earlier
-- run of a different revision of this file left #obl, #res or #key behind
-- with different columns, SQL Server binds the INSERT statements further
-- down against THAT table when it compiles the batch -- before the
-- CREATE TABLE below has had a chance to run -- and the load dies with
-- "Invalid column name" on a column this file plainly declares.
--
-- The GO after this block is what fixes it.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------
IF OBJECT_ID('tempdb..#obl') IS NOT NULL DROP TABLE #obl;
IF OBJECT_ID('tempdb..#res') IS NOT NULL DROP TABLE #res;
IF OBJECT_ID('tempdb..#key') IS NOT NULL DROP TABLE #key;
GO

CREATE TABLE #obl(
    seq INT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,
    practice_ref        NVARCHAR(100)  NOT NULL,
    practice_name       NVARCHAR(300)  NOT NULL,
    statement_reference NVARCHAR(100)  NOT NULL,
    obligation_name     NVARCHAR(500)  NOT NULL,
    obligation_desc     NVARCHAR(MAX)  NULL,
    keywords            NVARCHAR(MAX)  NULL,
    evidence_type       NVARCHAR(160)  NOT NULL,
    evidence_desc       NVARCHAR(MAX)  NULL,
    trigger_event   NVARCHAR(500)  NOT NULL,
    response_action NVARCHAR(1000) NOT NULL,
    sla_text        NVARCHAR(500)  NULL,
    sla_unit_text   NVARCHAR(120)  NULL,
    escalation_path NVARCHAR(500)  NULL);
GO

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------
-- Staged sheet rows
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT #obl(seq,practice_ref,practice_name,statement_reference,obligation_name,obligation_desc,keywords,evidence_type,evidence_desc,trigger_event,response_action,sla_text,sla_unit_text,escalation_path) VALUES
(1,N'POL-05',N'Review and improve policies',N'5.1',N'Review Policies After Significant Change',N'Respond by Review affected policies and update them where needed when When business strategy, technology, law, contract, risk, threat or incident lessons change significantly, led by Relevant Policy Owner with Information Security, within Within the risk-based review period. Covered event: Significant policy-related change. Frequency: On significant change. Evidence required: Event-driven policy review: Change or event, impact assessment, required updates, owner and decision.',N'significant change; business; technology; law; risk; threat; incident',N'Agreement',N'Evidence: Event-driven policy review. Details: Change or event, impact assessment, required updates, owner and decision',N'Significant policy-related change',N'Review affected policies and update them where needed',N'Within the risk-based review period',N'Not specified in source',NULL),
